[Handbook: Actinic keratoses and intraepithelial carcinoma / Bowen's disease]
Characterized by full-thickness epidermal dysplasia. Peak incidence in seventh decade. Only about $3-5%$ of plaques progress to SCC—-but the risk of metastasis from SCC is increased. Aetiologies:  chronic sun exposure (including sunbeds), radiation, oncogenic HPV (HPV-16 associated with anogenital, palmoplantar, and periungual SCC in situ), immunosuppression, and arsenic (lesions in sun-protected areas). Plaques are asymptomatic and enlarge slowly over years. Often misdiagnosed as ‘discoid eczema’ or ‘ringworm’ but fails to respond to treatment for these conditions.  Characterized by full-thickness epidermal dysplasia. Peak incidence in seventh decade. Only about $3-5\%$ of plaques progress to SCC—-but the risk of metastasis from SCC is increased. Aetiologies:  chronic sun exposure (including sunbeds), radiation, oncogenic HPV (HPV-16 associated with anogenital, palmoplantar, and periungual SCC in situ), immunosuppression, and arsenic (lesions in sun-protected areas). Plaques are asymptomatic and enlarge slowly over years. Often misdiagnosed as ‘discoid eczema’ or ‘ringworm’ but fails to respond to treatment for these conditions.
